<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#shirvanshah">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shirvanshah</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shirvanshah, &quot;king of Shirvan&quot;, was the title of the rulers of Shirvan in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. Used under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>, it was revived in 861 by the Arab governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haytham-ibn-khalid">Haytham ibn Khalid</a> and borne by his Yazidid descendants and later by the Derbendi line until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> annexed Shirvan in 1538. Their capitals were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shamakhi">Shamakhi</a> and, later, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shirvanshah.jpg" alt="Shirvanshah" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Caucasus topographic map-fr.svg by Bourrichon - fr:Bourrichon; This; derivative work by Don-kun · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caucasus_1311_AD_map_de_alt.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
